{"chunk_id": "Grapes_p0063_c0070", "doc_name": "Grapes.pdf", "doc_title": "AESA Based IPM Package - Grapes", "publisher": "DPPQS & NIPHM, Ministry of Agriculture, GoI", "year": 2014, "licence": "Government of India Public Domain", "page": 64, "language": "en", "crop_tags": ["general"], "disease_tags": ["general"], "has_dosage": false, "table_uncertain": false, "garbled": false, "no_text_layer": false, "text": "[Favourable conditions] 64 12. Botrytis bunch rot or gray mold: Disease symptoms:  One or more berries of a cluster show signs of decay just before harvest. The decay may progress to include most of the berries in a cluster. The infected fruit may become covered with a grayish-tan powder containing the spores of the fungus.  Berry stems and cluster stems may be invaded, causing them to shrivel.  When the fungus decays berries low in sugar, the rotting berry has a sour odor and taste.  If the berries are nearly mature and have a high sugar content, the decaying berry is quite firm, dry, and somewhat sweetish to the taste. Berries that have split or have been punctured often are attacked by other organisms, resulting in a sour or moldy decay. Survival and spread:  Fungus survives in all decaying vegetation. Its spores are present in the vine yard throughout the year. Favorable conditions:  Brown mold is most common when temperatures are higher than 30°C in the field or 24°C in storage.  Free moisture for six hours or longer on the surface is necessary for infection to occur. 1. 2. 3. Disease symptoms 1,2,3:https://www.google.co.in/search?q=botrytis+rot+of+grapes&espv=210&es_sm=93&source=lnms&tbm=isch&sa=X&ei=JXgMU 6SXKoeMrQe3iIDoBw&ved=0CAkQ_ *For management refer to page number----------------------- 13. Rust Disease symptoms:  The symptoms are in the form of numerous orange coloured pustules on the lower surface of the leaves. In case of severe infection such pustules cover the entire leaf surface leading to severe defoliation. Survival and spread:  The pathogens reproduce and survive in spots on leaves or stems and in fallen plant host debris. Favourable conditions:  Moisture and temperature above 20° C favours the development of disease.", "verbatim_text": "64 12. Botrytis bunch rot or gray mold: Disease symptoms:  One or more berries of a cluster show signs of decay just before harvest. The decay may progress to include most of the berries in a cluster. The infected fruit may become covered with a grayish-tan powder containing the spores of the fungus.  Berry stems and cluster stems may be invaded, causing them to shrivel.  When the fungus decays berries low in sugar, the rotting berry has a sour odor and taste.  If the berries are nearly mature and have a high sugar content, the decaying berry is quite firm, dry, and somewhat sweetish to the taste. Berries that have split or have been punctured often are attacked by other organisms, resulting in a sour or moldy decay. Survival and spread:  Fungus survives in all decaying vegetation. Its spores are present in the vine yard throughout the year. Favorable conditions:  Brown mold is most common when temperatures are higher than 30°C in the field or 24°C in storage.  Free moisture for six hours or longer on the surface is necessary for infection to occur. 1. 2. 3. Disease symptoms 1,2,3:https://www.google.co.in/search?q=botrytis+rot+of+grapes&espv=210&es_sm=93&source=lnms&tbm=isch&sa=X&ei=JXgMU 6SXKoeMrQe3iIDoBw&ved=0CAkQ_ *For management refer to page number----------------------- 13. Rust Disease symptoms:  The symptoms are in the form of numerous orange coloured pustules on the lower surface of the leaves. In case of severe infection such pustules cover the entire leaf surface leading to severe defoliation. Survival and spread:  The pathogens reproduce and survive in spots on leaves or stems and in fallen plant host debris. Favourable conditions:  Moisture and temperature above 20° C favours the development of disease.", "page_thumbnail_path": "kb\\pages\\Grapes\\page_0063.png"}
{"chunk_id": "Grapes_p0063_c0071", "doc_name": "Grapes.pdf", "doc_title": "AESA Based IPM Package - Grapes", "publisher": "DPPQS & NIPHM, Ministry of Agriculture, GoI", "year": 2014, "licence": "Government of India Public Domain", "page": 64, "language": "en", "crop_tags": ["general"], "disease_tags": ["general"], "has_dosage": false, "table_uncertain": false, "garbled": false, "no_text_layer": false, "text": "[Favourable conditions] pathogens reproduce and survive in spots on leaves or stems and in fallen plant host debris. Favourable conditions:  Moisture and temperature above 20° C favours the development of disease. 12. Botrytis bunch rot or gray mold:: Disease symptoms:  One or more berries of a cluster show signs of decay just before harvest. The decay may progress to include most of the berries in a cluster. The infected fruit may become covered with a grayish-tan powder containing the spores of the fungus.  Berry stems and cluster stems may be invaded, causing them to shrivel.  When the fungus decays berries low in sugar, the rotting berry has a sour odor and taste.  If the berries are nearly mature and have a high sugar content, the decaying berry is quite firm, dry, and somewhat sweetish to the taste. Berries that have split or have been punctured often are attacked by other organisms, resulting in a sour or moldy decay. Survival and spread:  Fungus survives in all decaying vegetation. Its spores are present in the vine yard throughout the year. Favorable conditions:  Brown mold is most common when temperatures are higher than 30°C in the field or 24°C in storage.  Free moisture for six hours or longer on the surface is necessary for infection to occur. 1. 2. 3. Disease symptoms 1,2,3:https://www.google.co.in/search?q=botrytis+rot+of+grapes&espv=210&es_sm=93&source=lnms&tbm=isch&sa=X&ei=JXgMU 6SXKoeMrQe3iIDoBw&ved=0CAkQ_ *For management refer to page number----------------------- 12. Botrytis bunch rot or gray mold:: 13. Rust", "verbatim_text": "pa