{"chunk_id": "Tomato_p0067_c0069", "doc_name": "Tomato.pdf", "doc_title": "AESA Based IPM Package - Tomato", "publisher": "DPPQS & NIPHM, Ministry of Agriculture, GoI", "year": 2014, "licence": "Government of India Public Domain", "page": 68, "language": "en", "crop_tags": ["general"], "disease_tags": ["general"], "has_dosage": false, "table_uncertain": false, "garbled": false, "no_text_layer": false, "text": "[Distribution and Identification] 68  The younger leaves may die in succession and the entire may wilt and die in a course of few days. Soon the petiole and the leaves droop and wilt.  In young plants, symptom consists of clearing of veinlet and dropping of petioles. In field, yellowing of the lower leaves first and affected leaflets wilt and die.  The symptoms continue in subsequent leaves. At later stage, browning of vascular system occurs. Plants become stunted and die. Survival and spread:  Soil and implements Favourable conditions:  Relatively high soil moisture and soil temperature *For management refer to page number----- X. Description of rodent pests 1) Lesser bandicoot: Distribution and Identification: Distributed throughout India and infests almost all crops. It is a robust rodent (200 to 300 g body weight) with a rounded head and a broad muzzle. Dorsum covered with grey-brownish rough hairs. Tail is naked, shorter than head and body. Breeds throughout the season and litter size 6-8 in normal conditions. Burrows are characterized by the presence of scooped soil at the entrance and mostly burrow openings are closed with soil. 1) Lesser bandicoot:: Distribution and Identification: Distributed throughout India and infests almost all crops. It is a robust rodent (200 to 300 g body weight) with a rounded head and a broad muzzle. Dorsum covered with grey-brownish rough hairs. Tail is naked, shorter than head and body. Breeds throughout the season and litter size 6-8 in normal conditions. Burrows are characterized by the presence of scooped soil at the entrance and mostly burrow openings are closed with soil.", "verbatim_text": "68  The younger leaves may die in succession and the entire may wilt and die in a course of few days. Soon the petiole and the leaves droop and wilt.  In young plants, symptom consists of clearing of veinlet and dropping of petioles. In field, yellowing of the lower leaves first and affected leaflets wilt and die.  The symptoms continue in subsequent leaves. At later stage, browning of vascular system occurs. Plants become stunted and die. Survival and spread:  Soil and implements Favourable conditions:  Relatively high soil moisture and soil temperature *For management refer to page number----- X. Description of rodent pests 1) Lesser bandicoot: Distribution and Identification: Distributed throughout India and infests almost all crops. It is a robust rodent (200 to 300 g body weight) with a rounded head and a broad muzzle. Dorsum covered with grey-brownish rough hairs. Tail is naked, shorter than head and body. Breeds throughout the season and litter size 6-8 in normal conditions. Burrows are characterized by the presence of scooped soil at the entrance and mostly burrow openings are closed with soil. 1) Lesser bandicoot:: Distribution and Identification: Distributed throughout India and infests almost all crops. It is a robust rodent (200 to 300 g body weight) with a rounded head and a broad muzzle. Dorsum covered with grey-brownish rough hairs. Tail is naked, shorter than head and body. Breeds throughout the season and litter size 6-8 in normal conditions. Burrows are characterized by the presence of scooped soil at the entrance and mostly burrow openings are closed with soil.", "page_thumbnail_path": "kb\\pages\\Tomato\\page_0067.png"}
{"chunk_id": "Tomato_p0068_c0070", "doc_name": "Tomato.pdf", "doc_title": "AESA Based IPM Package - Tomato", "publisher": "DPPQS & NIPHM, Ministry of Agriculture, GoI", "year": 2014, "licence": "Government of India Public Domain", "page": 69, "language": "en", "crop_tags": ["Tomato"], "disease_tags": ["general"], "has_dosage": false, "table_uncertain": false, "garbled": false, "no_text_layer": false, "text": "[3. Indian gerbil] 69 Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows. 2. House rat: Distributed throughout India. Medium sized (80-120g) slender rodent. Commonly found in houses and on plantation crops. Very good climber with longer tail than head and body. Occasionally causes damage to tomato in certain pockets. Inhabitation on trees and other places and won‟t make any burrows in fields 3. Indian gerbil: Distributed throughout the India. Inhabits rain-fed crop fields/ fallow/wastelands. Medium sized (100-250 g.) with light brownish dorsum and longer tail than head and body. The eyes are large, rounded ears and bicolour tail with terminal black tuft. The burrows have semi-circular openings with zigzag shape and 2 to 4 openings and emergency exits. Diseases cycles 1. Bacterial wilt: Ralstonia solanecearum Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows.: 2. House rat: Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows.: Distributed throughout India. Medium sized (80-120g) slender rodent. Commonly found in houses and on plantation crops. Very good climber with longer tail than head and body. Occasionally causes damage to tomato in certain pockets. Inhabitation on trees and other places and won‟t make any burrows in fields Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows.: 3. Indian gerbil:", "verbatim_text": "69 Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows. 2. House rat: Distributed throughout India. Medium sized (80-120g) slender rodent. Commonly found in houses and on plantation crops. Very good climber with longer tail than head and body. Occasionally causes damage to tomato in certain pockets. Inhabitation on trees and other places and won‟t make any burrows in fields 3. Indian gerbil: Distributed throughout the India. Inhabits rain-fed crop fields/ fallow/wastelands. Medium sized (100-250 g.) with light brownish dorsum and longer tail than head and body. The eyes are large, rounded ears and bicolour tail with terminal black tuft. The burrows have semi-circular openings with zigzag shape and 2 to 4 openings and emergency exits. Diseases cycles 1. Bacterial wilt: Ralstonia solanecearum Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows.: 2. House rat: Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows.: Distributed throughout India. Medium sized (80-120g) slender rodent. Commonly found in houses and on plantation crops. Very good climber with longer tail than head and body. Occasionally causes damage to tomato in certain pockets. Inhabitation on trees and other places and won‟t make any burrows in fields Damage symptoms: Mostly damage occurs at fruiting stage. Bandicoots cut the raw and ripened fruits and hoard them in their burrows.: 3. Indian gerbil:", "page_thumbnail_path": "kb\\pages\\Tomato\\page_0068.png"}
